<!--
  Suricata EVE-over-syslog decoder  (MutaSpace SOC lab, W4)

  fw-01 (OPNsense) runs Suricata with syslog_eve enabled, so it emits its EVE JSON
  alerts to syslog as program "suricata"; syslog-ng forwards that stream to the Wazuh
  manager's syslog <remote> listener on 10.10.10.20:514/udp (see 70-detections.yml and
  05-fw-config.yml).

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (the non-obvious part, proven on the live box 2026-07-24):
  Wazuh's built-in `json` decoder only fires on logs that BEGIN with '{'. A syslog-framed
  Suricata event does not - it is `<pri>timestamp host suricata[pid]: {json}`, so after the
  syslog pre-decoder extracts program_name="suricata" the built-in json decoder never
  matches, and `wazuh-logtest` reports "No decoder matched". Wazuh ships Suricata *rules*
  (0475-suricata_rules.xml, ids 86600+) but NO Suricata *decoder* for the syslog path - the
  shipped 86xxx rules assume the localfile/eve.json ingestion route where the json decoder is
  applied automatically. Forwarding EVE over syslog therefore needs this decoder.

  It is deliberately named "json" (a variant, scoped by program_name) so the decoded event is
  tagged `decoded_as json`, which is exactly the condition rule 86600 gates on:
      <rule id="86600" level="0"><decoded_as>json</decoded_as>...
  With this in place, testmynids across fw-01 produces rule 86601
  ("Suricata: Alert - GPL ATTACK_RESPONSE id check returned root", sid 2100498) in the
  Wazuh indexer. The program_name constraint keeps it from touching any non-Suricata log.
-->
<decoder name="json">
  <program_name>^suricata$</program_name>
  <plugin_decoder>JSON_Decoder</plugin_decoder>
</decoder>
